// Review of prisma/schema.prisma — only what this feature touches or is bound by.

// The header sets three rules this plan is constrained by. Quoted because two of
// them are cited in the plan as reasons.
//
//   "MONEY is stored as a whole number of centavos in an Int column."
//   "Derived on read, never stored: floating funds, a loan's OVERDUE state, and a
//    borrower's counted track record."
//   deletedAt is soft delete, mapped from a column still named archivedAt.
//
// IMPORTANT: the second line is what Loan.nextDueOn breaks. The plan proposes it
// anyway and says why; if the owner accepts, this header must gain the exception
// or it contradicts the column beneath it.

model Loan {
  capitalCentavos  Int
  interestBasis    InterestBasis @default(WEEKLY_RATE)
  borrowerRateBps  Int?          // NULL on FIXED_AMOUNT
  startOn          DateTime      @db.Date
  dueOn            DateTime      @db.Date
  termDays         Int           // NOTE: weeks are derived from this, never stored.
                                 // "there is no weeks column, because two columns
                                 // for one term can disagree". Every caller of
                                 // weeklySchedule must pass termDays / 7.
  interestCentavos Int
  totalCentavos    Int
  status           LoanStatus    @default(ACTIVE)

  payment  Payment?              // IMPORTANT: the to-one relation. It exists ONLY
                                 // because of the unique index below. Dropping
                                 // that index turns this into Payment[] and
                                 // breaks 21 read sites — all compile errors.

  @@index([userId, status, deletedAt])
  @@index([dueOn])               // NOTE: serves the date-range filter. Still does.
}

// From the model's own comment, and it is the rule the whole plan obeys:
//
//   "termDays, interestCentavos and totalCentavos are computed ONCE by
//    src/lib/money/ when the loan is created, and stored. Nothing recalculates
//    them afterwards."
//   "There is no OVERDUE value in LoanStatus. Overdue is ACTIVE with dueOn in the
//    past — a fact about today, not a state someone has to remember to write."

model Payment {
  loanId         String    @unique  // IMPORTANT: THE CONSTRAINT THAT CANNOT SURVIVE.
                                    // Measured on the live database 2026-09-24:
                                    //   Payment_loanId_key ON public."Payment"
                                    //     USING btree ("loanId")
                                    // A plain unique index, no predicate. There is
                                    // nothing to widen; it must be dropped and the
                                    // guarantee re-expressed as a PARTIAL unique
                                    // index on ("loanId") WHERE "weekNumber" IS NULL.
  paidOn         DateTime  @db.Date
  amountCentavos Int
  deletedAt      DateTime? @map("archivedAt")

  proofFiles ProofFile[]   // NOTE: THIS is why the plan generalises Payment rather
                           // than adding a LoanWeek table. A weekly payment gets
                           // proof, soft delete, Recently Deleted and purge for
                           // free by being a Payment. A parallel table would have
                           // needed all four rebuilt, since ProofFile.paymentId is
                           // an FK to this model.
}

// The model comment states the guarantee in words:
//   "loanId is unique: one payment per loan, enforced by the database rather than
//    by hoping the application never writes a second."
// The plan keeps that guarantee exactly, for the settling payment, via the partial
// index. It does not weaken it.

model LoanFunding {
  principalCentavos Int
  lenderRateBps     Int?  // per week, NULL on FIXED_AMOUNT
  adminCutBps       Int?
  earningsCentavos  Int   // IMPORTANT: what weeklySchedule slices. Stored at
  adminCutCentavos  Int   // creation, never recomputed. These two columns are the
                          // entire input to the weekly schedule — no rate is read.

  @@unique([loanId, lenderId])
}

// From its comment, the two invariants the plan's arithmetic inherits:
//   1. principalCentavos across a loan's rows sums to exactly Loan.capitalCentavos
//   2. lenderRateBps + adminCutBps == Loan.borrowerRateBps on every WEEKLY_RATE row
//
// IMPORTANT, and the plan's §1.2 rests on it:
//   "Earnings are stored rather than recomputed because the split's remainder
//    handling assigns leftover centavos deliberately; re-running it later against
//    a changed rate would move money that has already been paid out."
// Slicing the STORED earnings therefore inherits the guarantee that they sum to
// Loan.interestCentavos, which is what makes both weekly invariants hold at once.

enum InterestBasis { WEEKLY_RATE  FIXED_AMOUNT }
// NOTE: the plan adds InterestCollection beside this, deliberately as a SECOND
// enum rather than a third value here. FEATURES.md §5: "This is not a third
// interest basis." Adding WEEKLY here would make every switch on basis wrong.

// QUESTION: the enum comment says a FIXED_AMOUNT loan has every *RateBps column
// NULL, "Null rather than zero: a stored 0 renders as '0% a week' and reads as a
// rate somebody chose." interestCollection is proposed as NOT NULL with a default
// of AT_END, which is a real answer for every loan rather than an absent one — so
// the same reasoning points the other way here. Worth confirming.
